{
  "format": "WFScriptPanel",
  "version": 2,
  "description": "Six-page native AD3 hardware-loopback Test Station: real W1-to-Scope-CH1 capture, four C++ numeric limit tests, safe Cleanup, UUT identity, and automatically archived JSON reports.",
  "scriptName": "AD3HardwareTestStation.js",
  "activePageId": "uut",
  "lifecycle": {
    "initializeEnabled": false,
    "shutdownEnabled": false
  },
  "appearance": {
    "themeMode": "dark",
    "background": "",
    "showDesignGrid": true
  },
  "pages": [
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "name": "00 Wiring & Guide",
      "width": 1320,
      "height": 860,
      "controls": [
        {
          "id": "guideHeading",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "<div style=\"font-family:Segoe UI;color:#d9e8f4;font-size:13px\"><h3 style=\"color:#7dd3fc\">AD3 Hardware Loopback Test | WFScript v5.6.68</h3><p>A real Test Executive example built from previously validated Wavegen/Scope code.</p></div>",
          "x": 25,
          "y": 18,
          "width": 1264,
          "height": 77,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Rich Text",
            "wordWrap": true,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 8
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "guideWiring",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "<div style=\"font-family:Segoe UI;color:#d9e8f4;font-size:13px\"><h3 style=\"color:#7dd3fc\">1. Wiring (only two connections)</h3><p><b>W1 → Scope 1+</b> and <b>AD3 GND → Scope 1−</b>. Use two short jumper wires. Leave the tested circuit disconnected. This example generates a 1 kHz sine wave at 1.0 V peak, 0 V offset. Do not connect mains or other powered circuits.</p></div>",
          "x": 25,
          "y": 110,
          "width": 615,
          "height": 228,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Rich Text",
            "wordWrap": true,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 8
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "guideMode",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "<div style=\"font-family:Segoe UI;color:#d9e8f4;font-size:13px\"><h3 style=\"color:#7dd3fc\">2. Select the correct device</h3><p>Start with <b>Device → Simulation</b> to validate the test workflow. Stop Panel; select <b>Physical Device</b> with the Analog Discovery 3; confirm wiring before starting the Panel again. The JSON report identifies simulation versus hardware. Opening the Panel does NOT start W1.</p></div>",
          "x": 654,
          "y": 110,
          "width": 635,
          "height": 228,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Rich Text",
            "wordWrap": true,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 8
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "guideFlow",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "<div style=\"font-family:Segoe UI;color:#d9e8f4;font-size:13px\"><h3 style=\"color:#7dd3fc\">3. Real native Test Executive</h3><p><b>Setup:</b> configure W1 and Scope, start W1, then nonblocking 350 ms settling delay.<br><b>Main:</b> one Scope CH1 acquisition, then four numeric checks:<br>AC RMS 0.60–0.82 V, frequency 975–1025 Hz, peak-to-peak 1.70–2.30 V, DC mean −0.10–+0.10 V.<br><b>Cleanup:</b> stop Scope and W1, including the normal failure path. Limits and order are edited in <b>Test Sequence...</b>.</p></div>",
          "x": 25,
          "y": 350,
          "width": 615,
          "height": 341,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Rich Text",
            "wordWrap": true,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 8
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "guideNotes",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "<div style=\"font-family:Segoe UI;color:#d9e8f4;font-size:13px\"><h3 style=\"color:#7dd3fc\">4. Why the measurements belong to one capture</h3><p>The first numeric handler captures 16,384 samples at 1 MS/s; the next three handlers read the SAME saved acquisition. The C++ TestRunner, not JavaScript, evaluates configured numeric limits. The operator script only handles buttons and report display. <b>Do not use an emergency Abort to test normal Cleanup</b>; use the labelled graceful Stop action.</p></div>",
          "x": 654,
          "y": 350,
          "width": 635,
          "height": 341,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Rich Text",
            "wordWrap": true,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 8
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "guideFooter",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "<div style=\"font-family:Segoe UI;color:#d9e8f4;font-size:13px\"><h3 style=\"color:#7dd3fc\">5. Quick start</h3><p>Open <b>01 Run Information</b>, enter a UUT serial, choose <b>RUN TEST</b>, then view <b>02 Live Test</b>, <b>03 Measurements</b>, <b>04 Event Log</b> and <b>05 Run Report</b>. Auto-archived JSON is viewable in <b>Script → Test Results Browser</b>.</p></div>",
          "x": 25,
          "y": 703,
          "width": 1264,
          "height": 146,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Rich Text",
            "wordWrap": true,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 8
          }
        }
      ]
    },
